e election statistics】\nL85:   * 【77†Electoral boundaries】\nL86: \nL87:     * 【78†State districts】\nL88:     * 【79†State regions】\nL89:     * 【80†Local councils】\nL90:     * 【81†Council reviews】\nL91:       * 【82†Ward boundary reviews】\nL92:       * 【83†Electoral structure reviews】\nL93:     * 【84†What are electoral boundaries?】\nL94:     * 【85†Which boundaries cover where I live?】\nL95:     * 【86†State boundary reviews】\nL96:     * 【87†Download boundary maps】\nL97: \nL98:   * 【88†Candidates & Parties】\nL99:     * 【89†Become a state election candidate】\nL100:       * 【90†How to nominate】\nL101:       * 【91†Campaigning】\nL102:       * 【92†Authorising state election material】\nL103:       * 【93†How-to-vote card guidelines】\nL104:       * 【94†Election information sessions】\nL105:       * 【95†Appointing a scrutineer】\nL106:       * 【96†Information for scrutineers】\nL107:       * 【97†Upper house groups】\nL108:       * 【98†State election candidate resources】\nL109:     * 【99†Become a local council candidate】\nL110:       * 【100†How to nominate】\nL111:       * 【101†Authorising local council election material】\nL112:       * 【102†Appointing a scrutineer】\nL113:       * 【103†Information for scrutineers】\nL114:       * 【104†Local council candidate resources】\nL115:     * 【105†Registered political parties】\nL116:       * 【106†About registered political parties】\nL117:       * 【107†Register a political party】\nL118:       * 【108†Currently registered parties】\nL119:       * 【109†Party decisions and changes】\nL120:       * 【110†Party registration review】\nL121:     * 【111†Political donations】\nL122:       * 【112†Information for donors】\nL123:       * 【113†Information for recipients】\nL124:       * 【114†New entrants】\nL125:       * 【115†Indexation】\nL126:     * 【116†Funding】\nL127:       * 【117†Funding for registered political parties】\nL128:       * 【118†Funding for independent members】\nL129:       * 【119†Funding for independent candidates】\nL130:       * 【120†State campaign account】\nL131:       * 【121†Funding glossary】\nL132:     * 【122†Annual returns】\nL133:       * 【123†Registered political parties】\nL134:       * 【124†Associated entities】\nL135:       * 【125†Independents and groups】\nL136:       * 【126†Third-party campaigners】\nL137: 【127†Languages】 Toggle search\nL138: \nL139: Search\nL140: \nL141: Type a minimum of three characters then press UP or DOWN on the keyboard to navigate the autocompleted search results\nL142: \nL143: [Input] Search button [Button: Close search bar ]\nL144: ##\nL145: \nL146: 2026 state election - check or update your details before 8 pm on Tuesday 3 November.\nL147: \nL148: 【12†Check your enrolment】 【18†More state election information】\nL149: \nL150: ## On this page\nL151: # Counting the votes\nL152: \nL153: 【128†Listen†app-oc.readspeaker.com】\nL154: \nL155: When voting is finished, one of 2 counting systems is used to determine the results:\nL156: \nL157:   * preferential counting\nL158:   * proportional counting.\nL159: \nL160: We are required by law to count this way. Votes can be counted manually or by computer. You can read more in the 【129†Electoral Act 2002†www.legislation.vic.gov.au】 and 【130†Local Government Act 2020†www.legislation.vic.gov.au】.\nL161: ## Preferential vote counting\nL162: \nL163: The preferential counting system is used to count votes in the state Lower House and councils with single-councillor wards.\nL164: # How preferential votes are counted\nL165: \nL166: 【131†Video transcript】\nL167: \nL168: For the Lower House of State Parliament, and many local councils where you’re electing one councillor for your ward, the preferential voting system is used to count votes. This means that to be elected, a candidate must achieve an absolute majority of the total number of formal votes. In other words – more than 50% of the total formal votes.\nL169: \nL170: For example, if 20,000 votes are cast, the winner needs 10,001 votes.\nL171: The votes are allocated to each candidate based on the voter’s first preference – or where they have put the number one. After they have all been sorted, the number of votes for each candidate is counted. If no candidate has achieved an absolute majority (more than 50% ), a preference distribution is conducted.\nL172: To do this, the candidate with the lowest number of votes is excluded. The excluded candidate’s votes are inspected and allocated to the next preferred candidate - or where the voter has put the number two. After this, the totals are counted again. If there is still no absolute majority, the next lowest scoring candidate is excluded and, again, their votes are redistributed to where the voter placed the number two.\nL173: If the second preferenced candidate has already been excluded, then the third preference is considered, and so on.\nL174: This process is continued until one candidate receives an absolute majority of votes and is elected.\nL175: \nL176: So, make sure you have really thought about your preferences, because around half of the Lower House and council electorates are won on preferences!\nL177: \nL178: For further information, visit vec.vic.gov.au or call 131 832.\nL179: \nL180: When there is only one vacancy, so one person is elected, we use preferential counting.\nL181: Voters choose candidates by numbering all the boxes on a ballot paper in order of preference. This means voters rank all candidates in order of their preference, with 1 being their most preferred.\nL182: \nL183: To win, a candidate must have more than half (more than 50%) of all formal votes. This is known as an 'absolute majority'.\nL184: \nL185: If no candidate has an absolute majority, we conduct a preference distribution.\nL186: The candidate with the fewest votes is excluded and their votes are passed on to other candidates according to voters' preferences.\nL187: \nL188: This process is repeated until one candidate has an absolute majority.\nL189: ### Elections that use preferential counting\nL190: \nL191: Preferential counting is used in:\nL192: \nL193:   * the Victorian Parliament's Lower House\nL194:   * single-councillor wards in local councils\nL195:   * the Melbourne City Council leadership team.\nL196: \nL197: ## Proportional vote counting\nL198: \nL199: Proportional representation is a way of counting votes in an election when there is more than one person to be elected.\nL200: # How proportional representation works\nL201: \nL202: 【132†Video transcript】\nL203: \nL204: Proportional representation is a way of counting votes in an election when there is more than one candidate to be elected.\nL205: \nL206: The principle of proportional representation is that candidates are elected in proportion to their support from voters.\nL207: \nL208: In Victoria, we have proportional representation to elect members of the State's Upper House and many local councils.\nL209: \nL210: How do you vote?\nL211: You rank the candidates in the order you decide. Your preferences are vital in deciding who gets elected.\nL212: \nL213: To be elected, a candidate needs to get a proportion of votes, called the 'quota'. The quota varies according to the number of candidates to be elected. As an example, with three candidates to be elected and 140 votes, the quota is 36 votes.\nL214: \nL215: Here are the first preference votes for the six candidates.\nL216: \nL217: Belinda has more votes than the quota, and is elected.\nL218: Belinda's 'surplus' votes (the 12 votes over the quota) are distributed to the other candidates according to voters' preferences.\nL219: \nL220: Belinda's surplus votes don't lead to any other candidate reaching a quota.\nL221: \nL222: When you run out of surpluses, the candidate who now has the fewest votes (Emma) is excluded, and the votes for Emma go to the remaining candidates according to the voters' preferences.\nL223: \nL224: This continues until all the places for that electorate are filled.\nL225: Remember, with proportional representation, your preferences really count.\nL226: \nL227: For further information, visit vec.vic.gov.au or call 131 832.\nL228: \nL229: When more than one person is to be elected, we use proportional counting.\nL230: \nL231: To be elected, candidates must receive a proportion of votes known as the quota.\nL232: \nL233: The quota is calculated by dividing the total number of formal ballot papers by one more than the number of candidates to be elected, then adding one to the result (disregarding any remainder).\nL234: For example, if there were 140 formal votes and 3 candidates to be elected, the quota would be (140/(3+1))+1 = 36 votes.\nL235: \nL236: Any candidate who receives the quota is elected. When the number of votes for a candidate goes over the quota, and there are still vacancies to be filled, all votes for the candidate are distributed into the count at a reduced value, according to the voter’s next preference. This reduced value is called the transfer value.\nL237: The transfer value is calculated by dividing the number of votes over the quota by the number of ballot papers for the elected candidate. The transfer value is used to ensure surplus votes continue to have value in the count, while also maintaining the fairness of the count. Each ballot paper has the individual preferences of a voter, and there is no way to equitably separate votes that count towards a quota and those that are surplus.\nL238: Instead, all preference votes for a candidate elected above a quota are distributed in the count at a reduced value, meaning they are worth a fraction of a whole vote.\nL239: For example, if there were 140 formal votes and 3 candidates to be elected:\nL240: \nL241:   * then the quota is 36 votes.\nL242:   * If candidate A receives 40 first preference votes, they are elected, and\nL243:   * there is a surplus of 4 votes (40-36=4).\nL244:   * The transfer value is 0.1 (4/40=0.1), and\nL245:   * the 40 votes are then distributed to other candidates based on each voter’s second preference at a value of 0.1 per vote.\nL246: The transfer value ensures the elected candidate retains the value of the quota as well as ensures the surplus votes are included while maintaining the fair distribution of all voters’ preferences as the count continues.\nL247: If no candidates reach the quota, or if there are still vacancies after all the votes over the quota have been passed on, the candidate with the fewest votes is excluded. Their votes are passed on to other candidates according to voters’ preferences, at the same value as they were received by the excluded candidate. The process is repeated until all vacancies are filled.\nL248: ### Elections that use proportional counting\nL249: \nL250: Proportional counting is used in:\nL251: \nL252:   * the Victorian Parliament's Upper House\nL253:   * multi-councillor wards\nL254:   * unsubdivided councils\nL255:   * Melbourne City Council councillors.\nL256: ## Computerised calculation\nL257: \nL258: ComputerCount lets us hold computerised counts for certain elections in the presence of scrutineers (if appointed). This software can calculate a result for both preferential and proportional representation counts.\nL259: The ComputerCount functionality was initially implemented in 2000 and is fully developed and maintained by the VEC. The ComputerCount code and functionality has been independently audited (most recently in 2024) to meet the requirements for the election set out in the:\nL260:   * 【129†Electoral Act 2002†www.legislation.vic.gov.au】, sections 114, 114A and 115\nL261:   * 【130†Local Government Act 2020†www.legislation.vic.gov.au】, section 263 and Divisions 6 and 7 of Part 8\nL262:   * 【133†City of Melbourne Act 2001†www.legislation.vic.gov.au】, section 18.\nL263: \nL264: Click on the links to view C# source code used in the ComputerCount module (formatted as text).\nL265: \nL266:   * 【134†DComputerCount.txt】\nL267:   * 【135†CalculationEngine.txt】\nL268:   * 【136†CalculateWorkflow.txt】\nL269:   * 【137†Calculate.txt】\nL270: \nL271: Audit report download:\nL272:   * 【138†2024 ComputerCount audit report】\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPACKING Definition & Meaning - Merriam-Webster (https://www.merriam-webster.com/dictionary/packing)\nciteturn33view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn31view0\",\"id\":19}); Total lines: 212\nL0: 【0†iframe†www.googletagmanager.com】 【1†iframe†www.googletagmanager.com】\nL1: \nL2: [Input: Search Dictionary]\nL3: \nL4:   * 【2†Games】\nL5:   * 【3†Word of the Day】\nL6:   * 【4†Grammar】\nL7:   * 【5†Word Finder】\nL8:   * 【6†Slang】\nL9:   * 【7†Newsletters】\nL10:   * 【8†Wordplay】\nL11:   * 【9†Rhymes】\nL12:   * 【10†Thesaurus】\nL13:   * 【11†Join MWU†unabridged.merriam-webster.com】\nL14:   * More\nL15:     * 【2†Games】\nL16:     * 【3†Word of the Day】\nL17:     * 【4†Grammar】\nL18:     * 【8†Wordplay】\nL19:     * 【6†Slang】\nL20:     * 【9†Rhymes】\nL21:     * 【5†Word Finder】\nL22:     * 【7†Newsletters】\nL23:     * 【10†Thesaurus】\nL24:     * 【11†Join MWU†unabridged.merriam-webster.com】\nL25:     * Shop\nL26:     * 【12†Books†shop.merriam-webster.com】\nL27:     * 【13†Merch†merriamwebster.threadless.com】\nL28: \nL29: 【14†Dictionary】 Definition\nL30: \nL31:   * # packing\nL32: \nL33: 1 of 2\nL34: ## 【15†noun】\nL35: \nL36: pack·​ing 【16†ˈpa-kiŋ 】\nL37: \nL38: 【17†Synonyms of packing】\nL39: \nL40: 1\nL41: \nL42: a\nL43: \nL44: : the action or process of packing something\nL45: \nL46: also : a method of packing\nL47: \nL48: b\nL49: \nL50: : the processing of food and especially meat for future sale\nL51: \nL52: 2\nL53: \nL54: : material (such as a covering or stuffing) used to protect 【18†packed】 goods (as for shipping)\nL55: \nL56: also : material used for making airtight or watertight\nL57: \nL58: packing for a faucet\nL59: \nL60: 3\nL61: US politics : a form of 【19†gerrymandering】 in which election districts are drawn in such a way that voters likely to support the opposing party are concentrated into a single district to limit the influence of their votes in other districts\nL62: … it calculates the impact of the two mechanisms that create gerrymandering. The first of these is \"packing,\" which means one side's votes have been overconcentrated so they win by huge margins in a small number of districts (creating a surplus of votes).—G. Terry Madonna and Michael Young\nL63: \nL64: compare 【20†cracking sense 2】\nL65: \nL66: packing\nL67: \nL68: 2 of 2\nL69: \nL70: present participle of 【21†pack entry 2】, 【22†pack entry 3】\nL71: ## Examples of packing in a Sentence\nL72: \nL73: Noun We finished all our packing yesterday. I used some old newspapers for packing.\nL74: \nL75: Recent Examples on the Web\nL76: \nL77: Examples are automatically compiled from online sources to show current usage. 【14†Read More】 Opinions expressed in the examples do not represent those of Merriam-Webster or its editors. 【23†Send us feedback†docs.google.com】.\nL78: \nL79: Noun\nL80: Dealers will be allowed to bring packing crates into the Grand Palais after the fair closes Friday, but no one will be permitted inside on Saturday, sources said. — Devorah Lauter, ARTnews.com, 22 Oct. 2026 Pack smarter and travel happier with a set of three packing cubes in beautiful, coordinating Indigo patterns to keep clothes, shoes, accessories and travel items organized and easy to find. — ABC News, 17 Oct.\nL81: 2026 This 20,000-square-foot campus in Englewood, housed in a former mattress factory, will have much more space for food packing and cold storage. — Kathleen Toner, CNN Money, 16 Oct. 2026 Commercial invoices, packing lists, bills of lading and origin documentation should be consistent, while classification decisions should be supported by information sufficient to explain and substantiate them. — Shannon Hynds, Footwear News, 15 Oct. 2026 【24†See All Example Sentences for packing】\nL82: ## Word History\nL83: \nL84: First Known Use\nL85: \nL86: Noun\nL87: \nL88: 14th c